---
/**
 * Article under the image translator. Wording follows the corrected source.
 * Pandoc backslashes are not part of the Morse.
 */
const link = 'text-accent underline underline-offset-4';
const h2 = 'text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight';
const h3 = 'text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight';
const tableClass = 'w-full border-collapse text-left text-base';
const thClass = 'border-b border-line bg-surface px-3 py-2 font-semibold';
const tdClass = 'border-b border-line px-3 py-2 align-top';
const code = 'font-mono tracking-wide';
---

<article class="mt-12 grid gap-10 rounded-2xl border border-line px-4 py-6 text-[1.0625rem] leading-7 sm:px-8 sm:py-8">
  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>About Morse Code Image Translator</h2>
    <p>
      Morse code Image Translator is an online Morse code image decoder tool that analyze, recognize and extract the
      pattern with accuracy from the Image you upload. Pictures can be from screenshots, flashcards, traditional
      documents, and images of dots of dashes too instead of you type by your own hands manually. This tool looks and
      reads the width of each mark in your image and figures out which ones are dots and which are dashes.
    </p>
    <p>
      You might have Morse in a photo of an old telegram, a page from a radio handbook, a screenshot from a puzzle, or a
      worksheet from a Morse class. All of these work the same way: the tool looks at each mark on its own, not at the
      words around it. The whole process runs inside your browser locally, so your image never leaves your device. If
      your Morse is sound instead of a picture, like a radio recording or a beep you captured on your phone, our
      <a class={link} href="/morse-code-audio-translator/">Morse code audio translator</a> reads that the same way, right
      in your browser.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>How to use Morse code Image Translator?</h2>
    <p>
      Using Morse Code Image Translator is very easy and simple because it's user friendly and fast, we will guide you
      in four simple steps below.
    </p>
    <ol class="grid list-decimal gap-3 pl-5">
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold">Upload your image:</span> Drag and drop it in, or click to browse. PNG, JPG, WEBP, GIF,
        and BMP all work.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold">The tool will scan it:</span> It looks at the marks in your image and works out which
        ones are dots and which are dashes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold">Check the detected Morse:</span> You will see the dots and dashes it found, in a box
        you can edit. If a mark was misread, fix it there and the text will update.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold">Read, copy, listen, or download.</span> The decoded text appears next to the Morse.
        Copy it, play it as audio to hear it the way it would sound as a real signal, or download the result as a text
        file or a WAV audio file.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>Why Image Decoding Is Trickier Than It Looks</h2>
    <p>
      Morse code was built for sound. Every dot and dash is a length of time, measured against one unit: the length of
      a single dot. A dash is three units. The gap between letters is three units, and the gap between words is seven.
      On a radio or a telegraph line, those lengths are real and steady, no matter how fast or slow someone sends.
    </p>
    <p>
      A picture freezes that timing into space. A tone that lasts 100 milliseconds becomes a mark that is, say, 12
      pixels wide. The tool now has to look at that width and guess whether it was a dot or a dash, based only on how
      it compares to the marks around it.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is where things get fragile. A blurry photo, bad lighting, or a low-resolution scan can all stretch or
      shrink those widths. A dot that runs slightly wide in a blurry shot can look like a dash. Two dots sitting close
      together can blur into what looks like one long mark. None of this happens with a clean audio signal, where the
      timing is exact.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is the real reason a clear, high-contrast image decodes so much better than a dim photo taken at an angle.
      It is not about luck. It is about how reliably the tool can measure something that was never meant to sit still.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>What This Image Decoder Can Do</h2>
    <p>
      Every image tool makes trade-offs between formats, accuracy, and privacy. Here is what this one actually does,
