    <h3 class={h3}>Why this matters more than it sounds</h3>
    <p>
      Two of these points are worth a closer look, because most Morse image tools get them wrong in one direction or
      another.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="font-semibold">The editable Morse field solves the biggest complaint with image decoding.</span> As
      explained above, a blurry mark or a low-contrast photo can shift a dot into looking like a dash. Most tools just
      hand you a wrong answer and leave you to re-upload a better photo and hope. Letting you fix the detected Morse
      directly means one bad character does not force you to start over.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="font-semibold">The privacy point is not the same for every tool on the market.</span> Some image
      decoders send your photo to a cloud AI service to read it, which means the image leaves your device before you
      ever see a result. That may be fine for a random puzzle screenshot, but it is worth knowing before you upload
