      scanning documents in this tool.
    </p>
    <h3 class={h3}>For Educational Purposes</h3>
    <p>
      Students who are learning morse code, can take pictures of their practice sheet and upload in the Morse Code
      image decoder. From this they can check their accuracy and learn morse code faster instead of comparing it with a
      Morse Code chart. Quick feedback like this has long been part of Morse training, since confirming each answer
      right away helps mistakes get corrected before they turn into a habit.
    </p>
    <h3 class={h3}>For HAM Radio Operators</h3>
    <p>
      HAM radio operators also use this Morse code image translator for various purposes, such as decoding QSL cards,
      signal waveform diagrams, contest logs, and printed training sheets. These often exist only as scans or photos,
      so reading them by hand takes time that this tool skips.
    </p>
    <h3 class={h3}>For Puzzle Solvers</h3>
    <p>
      Our Morse Code image decoder is beneficial for puzzle solvers. They can use this for decode Morse Code symbols
      that appear in Room puzzles, online game riddles, ARG ( Alternate Reality games ) challenges. They can use this
      tool as a secret weapon to get competitive advantage.
    </p>
    <h3 class={h3}>For Anyone Who's Curious</h3>
    <p>
      Whenever you see images, an old letter with Morse Code and you think about it, and can't decode it manually. In
      this situation you can easily decode it with a single click, by using our Morse Code image decoder.
    </p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Emergency Basics</h3>
    <p>
      <a class={link} href="/sos-in-morse-code/">SOS</a> and a few other simple signals are still worth knowing by
      sight. If you come across Morse in a photo of a training poster, a safety card, or an old emergency guide, you
      can upload it here to check what it says.
    </p>
    <h3 class={h3}>For Everyday Screenshots</h3>
    <p>
      Not every Morse image comes from a puzzle or an archive. Sometimes it is a screenshot from a social media post,
      an inbox chat, or someone's practice app, or a few dots and dashes drawn on a whiteboard or a flashcard. This
      tool reads those the same way it reads any other image.
    </p>
