            <td class={tdClass}>A busy or patterned background</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class={tdClass}>Even spacing between marks</td>
            <td class={tdClass}>Marks that touch or overlap</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class={tdClass}>Strong contrast, dark on light or light on dark</td>
            <td class={tdClass}>Grey-on-white or similar low-contrast tones</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class={tdClass}>Only real Morse marks</td>
            <td class={tdClass}>Decorative symbols that are not standard Morse</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <h3 class={h3}>How the Image Was Made</h3>
    <p>
      In this list it matters more than anything else. A dot ( . ) and a dash ( - ) are only told apart by their width,
      so anything that keeps that width consistent will decode better than anything that does not.
    </p>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-2 pl-5">
      <li>Marks made by a program, with fixed and even spacing, read the most reliably.</li>
      <li>
        Morse typed out in a regular font often fails, because fonts space characters unevenly and the tool cannot
        always tell a gap inside a letter from a gap between letters.
      </li>
      <li>
        Always keep gaps clearly different in size: the smallest gap inside a letter, a wider one between letters, and
        the widest between words.
      </li>
      <li>
        Leave open space above and below the row. A line, a border, or an underline touching the marks will blend into
        them and throw off the reading.
      </li>
      <li>Always keep the code on one straight line, without any overlap.</li>
      <li>
        If a word comes out wrong after decoding, it is usually one gap, too wide or too narrow. Fixing the detected
        Morse manually, one space at a time, usually solves it.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>Some Examples</h2>
    <p>Try these examples to see how the tool reads a clean image.</p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class={tableClass}>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class={thClass} scope="col">Image shows</th>
            <th class={thClass} scope="col">Decoded text</th>
            <th class={thClass} scope="col">What it shows</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>.-- --- .-. .-.. -..</td>
            <td class={tdClass}>WORLD</td>
            <td class={tdClass}>A single word, no slashes needed</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>... --- ...</td>
            <td class={tdClass}>SOS</td>
            <td class={tdClass}>The shortest, most recognizable signal in Morse</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>.... .. / - .... . .-. .</td>
            <td class={tdClass}>HI THERE</td>
            <td class={tdClass}>A slash marking the gap between two words</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>--. --- --- -.. / -- --- .-. -. .. -. --.</td>
            <td class={tdClass}>GOOD MORNING</td>
            <td class={tdClass}>A longer message across two words</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      <span class="font-semibold">A note on the slash:</span> In a printed or digital image, a forward slash ( / ) is
      often used to mark a word gap, because a picture cannot show a pause in time. In a real, timed signal, there is
      no slash - the word gap is just a longer silence, and that silence (a wide empty gap) is what this tool actually
      looks for. If your image shows the word gap as plain empty space, the tool finds it correctly on its own. If
      your image instead has an actual slash character drawn between words, the tool reads it as a stray mark, not as
      a separator - crop it out or replace it with a wider gap before uploading.
    </p>
  </section>

