          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th class={thClass} scope="row">Best suited for</th>
            <td class={tdClass}>A messy or unfamiliar image, or a quick check</td>
            <td class={tdClass}>Building real fluency in Morse</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th class={thClass} scope="row">Cost</th>
            <td class={tdClass}>Free</td>
            <td class={tdClass}>Free, but takes your time</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Use the decoder when the source is a picture and speed matters. Reach for your own eyes and ears when the goal is
      learning Morse itself, since that skill only grows with practice, not with a shortcut.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>Limitations of our Tool</h2>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-2 pl-5">
      <li>Our tool only decodes clear and clean images. A faint, blurry, or low-contrast mark can be misread, as explained above.</li>
      <li>Handwritten and decorative Morse work only when each dot and dash is clearly separated; cursive or stylized marks often fail.</li>
      <li>Our tool reads only International Morse code. It does not handle American Morse, or any made-up or proprietary pictogram system.</li>
      <li>This tool does not replace checking critical or time-sensitive messages by another method. Always confirm anything important before acting on it.</li>
      <li>This tool reads one single row of marks. It does not read Morse code split across multiple lines.</li>
      <li>For an animated GIF, only the first frame is read. If your Morse is on a later frame, use a still screenshot of that frame instead.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>Common Problems and How to Fix Them</h2>
    <p>If something does not look right, the cause is almost always the image, not the tool. Start here before trying a different photo.</p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Image Is Not Uploading</h3>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-2 pl-5">
      <li>Confirm the file is one of the formats this tool reads: PNG, JPG, WEBP, GIF, or BMP.</li>
      <li>Check the file is not corrupted or only partly downloaded. Try opening it in your photo viewer first.</li>
      <li>If it still fails, try a different browser or refresh the page and upload again.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3 class={h3}>The Decoded Text Looks Wrong</h3>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-2 pl-5">
      <li>Open the editable Morse field and compare it against what you can see in the image. Most wrong results come down to one or two misread marks, not the whole thing.</li>
      <li>Fix any wrong dot or dash directly, and the text updates right away, no need to re-upload.</li>
      <li>If many marks look wrong, the image itself is likely the problem. Check it against the tips above: low contrast, blur, and uneven spacing are the usual causes.</li>
      <li>Confirm the source is actually standard Morse. Puzzle makers sometimes use lookalike symbols that are not real Morse code.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3 class={h3}>Only Part of the Message Decoded</h3>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-2 pl-5">
      <li>This is usually a lighting or contrast problem in one part of the image, not the whole thing.</li>
      <li>Crop the image down to just the Morse code, removing borders, menus, or backgrounds around it.</li>
      <li>Check the edges of the photo. Marks that are cut off or run outside the frame will not be read.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3 class={h3}>Nothing Decodes At All</h3>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-2 pl-5">
      <li>Confirm the image actually contains Morse code, not a drawing or icon that looks similar.</li>
      <li>Faint, very low-contrast, or heavily stylized marks may not register as marks at all. Try a sharper or higher-contrast version if you have one.</li>
      <li>A screenshot of the same content, instead of a photo, often works when a photo does not.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3 class={h3}>Decoding Is Slow</h3>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-2 pl-5">
      <li>Large image files take longer to process. A smaller file, or a tighter crop, speeds this up.</li>
      <li>Crop out everything except the Morse code before uploading. Less image means less to scan.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>Morse Code Timing Reference</h2>
    <p>
      This is the same timing that makes image decoding possible in the first place, from the "Why Image Decoding Is
      Trickier Than It Looks" section above. Every length is a multiple of one unit, the length of a single dot.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class={tableClass}>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class={thClass} scope="col">Part</th>
            <th class={thClass} scope="col">Length</th>
            <th class={thClass} scope="col">At 12 WPM</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>Dot</td><td class={tdClass}>1 unit</td><td class={tdClass}>100 ms</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>Dash</td><td class={tdClass}>3 units</td><td class={tdClass}>300 ms</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>Gap inside a letter</td><td class={tdClass}>1 unit</td><td class={tdClass}>100 ms</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>Gap between letters</td><td class={tdClass}>3 units</td><td class={tdClass}>300 ms</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>Gap between words</td><td class={tdClass}>7 units</td><td class={tdClass}>700 ms</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      These ratios come from the ITU standard, ITU-R M.1677-1. In an image, this timing becomes pixel width instead of
      sound duration, but the math stays the same. A dash is always three times the width of a dot, no matter how large
      or small the image is.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>Morse Code Chart (Letters, Numbers, and Punctuation)</h2>
    <p>Use this chart to double-check anything the decoder gives you, or to read Morse yourself without the tool.</p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Letters</h3>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class={tableClass}>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class={thClass} scope="col">Letter</th>
            <th class={thClass} scope="col">Morse</th>
            <th class={thClass} scope="col">Letter</th>
            <th class={thClass} scope="col">Morse</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>A</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>.-</td><td class={tdClass}>N</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>-.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>B</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>-...</td><td class={tdClass}>O</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>---</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>C</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>-.-.</td><td class={tdClass}>P</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>.--.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>D</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>-..</td><td class={tdClass}>Q</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>--.-</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>E</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>.</td><td class={tdClass}>R</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>.-.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>F</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>..-.</td><td class={tdClass}>S</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>...</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>G</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>--.</td><td class={tdClass}>T</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>-</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>H</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>....</td><td class={tdClass}>U</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>..-</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>I</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>..</td><td class={tdClass}>V</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>...-</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>J</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>.---</td><td class={tdClass}>W</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>.--</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>K</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>-.-</td><td class={tdClass}>X</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>-..-</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>L</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>.-..</td><td class={tdClass}>Y</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>-.--</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>M</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>--</td><td class={tdClass}>Z</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>--..</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <h3 class={h3}>Numbers</h3>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class={tableClass}>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class={thClass} scope="col">Number</th>
            <th class={thClass} scope="col">Morse</th>
            <th class={thClass} scope="col">Number</th>
            <th class={thClass} scope="col">Morse</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>0</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>-----</td><td class={tdClass}>5</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>.....</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>1</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>.----</td><td class={tdClass}>6</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>-....</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>2</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>..---</td><td class={tdClass}>7</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>--...</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>3</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>...--</td><td class={tdClass}>8</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>---..</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>4</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>....-</td><td class={tdClass}>9</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>----.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Notice the pattern: every digit's code is five marks long. For 1 through 5, the number of dots at the start
      equals the digit, with dashes filling out the rest — 1 is one dot and four dashes, 5 is five dots. For 6 through
      9, dashes lead instead: the count of leading dashes is five less than the digit, with dots filling out the rest —
      6 is one dash and four dots, 9 is four dashes and one dot. 0 is five dashes, the mirror of 5's five dots.
    </p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Punctuation</h3>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class={tableClass}>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class={thClass} scope="col">Mark</th>
            <th class={thClass} scope="col">Morse</th>
            <th class={thClass} scope="col">Mark</th>
            <th class={thClass} scope="col">Morse</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>. (period)</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>.-.-.-</td><td class={tdClass}>: (colon)</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>---...</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>, (comma)</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>--..--</td><td class={tdClass}>; (semicolon)</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>-.-.-.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>? (question mark)</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>..--..</td><td class={tdClass}>= (equals)</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>-...-</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>' (apostrophe)</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>.----.</td><td class={tdClass}>+ (plus)</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>.-.-.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>! (exclamation)</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>-.-.--</td><td class={tdClass}>- (hyphen)</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>-....-</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>/ (slash)</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>-..-.</td><td class={tdClass}>_ (underscore)</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>..--.-</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>( (open parenthesis)</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>-.--.</td><td class={tdClass}>" (quotation mark)</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>.-..-.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>) (close parenthesis)</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>-.--.-</td><td class={tdClass}>$ (dollar sign)</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>...-..-</td></tr>
          <tr><td class={tdClass}>&amp; (ampersand)</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>.-...</td><td class={tdClass}>@ (at sign)</td><td class={`${tdClass} ${code}`}>.--.-.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      A few of these, like $ and the underscore, are rare outside amateur radio. If you only remember a handful, the
      period, comma, and question mark cover almost everything you will actually run into.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cv cv-faq grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="grid gap-4">
      <div class="grid gap-2">
        <h3 class={h3}>My image has dots and dashes, but when I check it against a Morse chart, nothing makes sense. Why?</h3>
        <p>
          It is almost always a timing problem, not a reading problem. In real Morse, a dash is always three times as
          long as a dot, and the gaps between letters and words follow their own fixed ratios. Puzzle makers and
          hand-drawn images do not always stick to this. If the proportions in your image are inconsistent, even a
          careful manual count will produce garbage. Let the decoder measure the widths for you, then check the result
          in the editable field rather than counting by eye.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid gap-2">
        <h3 class={h3}>How do I tell a dot from a dash when they look almost the same length?</h3>
        <p>
          By eye, this is genuinely hard, especially in a blurry or low-resolution photo. The decoder does not guess
          from a fixed ruler. It compares every mark in the image to the others and classifies the short ones as dots
          and the long ones as dashes, relative to each other. That is also why a tightly cropped, high-contrast image
          works better than a wide photo with lots of empty space around the code.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid gap-2">
        <h3 class={h3}>Is this actually Morse code, or could it be something else?</h3>
        <p>
          Not every dot-and-dash pattern in a puzzle is the real Morse. Semaphore, Braille, and invented ciphers can
          look similar at a glance. If this tool returns nothing readable, or only a string of single letters with no
          real words, that is a sign the source may not follow International Morse rules at all. Compare a few
          characters against the chart above as a quick sanity check.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid gap-2">
        <h3 class={h3}>I see a forward slash ( / ) in my Morse. Is that a real part of the code?</h3>
        <p>
          No. A slash is a printed convention to show a word gap, since a still image cannot show a pause in time. In
          an actual transmitted signal, there is no symbol at all, just a longer silence - and that longer silence is
          what this tool looks for. If your image shows the word gap as plain empty space, the tool finds it on its
          own. If your image shows an actual slash character drawn between the words, the tool reads it as a stray
          mark rather than a separator, so it is best to crop it out or widen the gap in its place before uploading.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid gap-2">
        <h3 class={h3}>Why does my result have extra or missing letters?</h3>
        <p>
          This almost always comes down to one gap being measured as slightly too wide or too narrow. A gap that
          should separate two letters can get read as a letter-internal gap, or the other way around. Open the
          editable Morse field, compare it to the image, and adjust the spacing around the character that looks wrong.
          You do not need to re-upload anything.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid gap-2">
        <h3 class={h3}>Can I upload a photo I took with my phone, or does it need to be a clean screenshot?</h3>
        <p>
          Both work, but not equally well. A screenshot or a saved image usually decodes better, because there is no
          camera angle, blur, or lighting to correct for. A phone photo can still work well if it is sharp, evenly
          lit, and shot straight on rather than at an angle.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid gap-2">
        <h3 class={h3}>Is my image uploaded anywhere, or does it stay on my device?</h3>
        <p>
          Everything runs in your browser. Your image is never sent to a server, so nothing about it leaves your
          device at any point.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid gap-2">
        <h3 class={h3}>Does this work for handwritten Morse, like dots and dashes drawn by hand?</h3>
        <p>
          It can, but results vary more than with printed or digital marks. A hand-drawn gap is rarely as consistent
          as a computer-generated one, and that inconsistency is exactly what the tool uses to tell a letter apart
          from another. Clear, evenly spaced handwriting works better than quick or cramped writing.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid gap-2">
        <h3 class={h3}>What if only part of my image decodes and the rest is blank?</h3>
        <p>
          This usually means one section of the image has worse lighting or contrast than the rest, not that the tool
          stopped partway through. Crop the image down to just the row of Morse code, and check that nothing is cut
          off at the edges of the photo.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid gap-2">
        <h3 class={h3}>Can this tool decode something that is not real Morse code, like a made-up puzzle cipher?</h3>
        <p>
          No, and it should not try to. It is built to recognize the timing and spacing rules of International Morse
          code specifically. A cipher that reuses the look of dots and dashes but follows its own invented rules needs
          to be solved by hand, or decoded against whatever system the puzzle itself defines.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</article>
